 "reading": "What the tags say. The defects that cost words on the 96 are framing and material, not optics, as L-capture expected from the six worst pages. Median over the 29 routes, against pages of the same kind and source without the tag: edge cut -6.4 NES points (26 of 29 routes worse), coloured stock or a colour cast -4.1 (27/29), mixed content -3.0 (26/29; the two severe pages -20.4), neighbouring material in frame -1.9 (21/29; the 18 severe, where it carries readable text, -5.2), faint ink -1.8 (20/29), show-through -0.7 (21/29; the 12 severe -5.2). The optical tags show no loss: perspective +1.1, glare +2.0, defocus +2.2, shadow +2.5, curl +2.6, hand in frame +3.2, low light +9.0 on 3 pages, and no page shows motion blur. The cheap and small readers carry most of the spread (Apple Vision -31 on faint ink, -24 on glare; the strongest cloud readers lose 1 to 3 points on any tag). Small groups: glare, defocus, shadow, hand and low light are on 3 to 9 pages each, so their signs are not settled. The 20 ladder pages are among the 96 and carry the same tags. On them the quarter-size copies lose most on the 5 defocus-tagged pages (44 points against 12, pooled), but that is native size, not focus: four of the five are PDF images 1,255 to 2,538 px long, which collapse at a quarter whatever their tags (the untagged 1,297 px page loses a median 98 points across routes), and the one defocus-tagged phone original loses nothing. No ladder tag effect is separable from native size on 20 pages."
}
